================================================================================
PART C  what each red proves, and the one case that has none
================================================================================
RED 1, the pre-merge gate. With the gate reverted to admit only PASS and XFAIL, a parked deferred red is skipped:
the case reports verdict NOT_RUN with fcov status None, and the summary case reports not_run=1 where it wants the
regraded verdict. Both go BAD. With the gate widened both pass.

RED 2, the unchecked-fixture verdict. With the choice reverted to grading an unchecked fixture, the first case
reports FAIL where it wants NOT_RUN and goes BAD, while the other two cases stay ok, which is the point: reverting
this one rule moves exactly one case and leaves the undeclared-reason and collected-failure rules alone.

RED 3, the pre-grading restore. With the restore removed, a deferred red carrying a real simulation failure comes
out of the pre-merge pass still NOT_RUN, carrying the missing-check reason instead of its own failure. So without
the restore the pass grades from the parked verdict and a genuine crash is regraded away, which is what the
Orchestrator asked to see pinned. With the restore in place the same case reads "red fixture failed for an
undeclared reason: sv_fatal at sim.log:41", keeping the collected reason.

THE GREENS RESTORE THE COMMITTED BYTES, which is how this file shows the reverts left nothing behind: each GREEN
section prints the hash of the file it just restored, and those hashes are the parent commit's own blobs.

  5c9869bbe078  e988ee6:dv/auto_dv/flow/gen_regress.py
  6d20a0b5dc32  e988ee6:dv/auto_dv/flow/gen_run.py

THE CASE WITH NO RED, stated rather than faked. "An unchecked deferred red counts as NOT_RUN, never as a pass or a
red-ok, and stays in the pass-rate denominator" tests gen_regress.summarize, which this work never changed. It is a
reliance check on unchanged behaviour: the new NOT_RUN verdict depends on that accounting, so it is pinned, but a
red for it would mean reverting a rule nobody here wrote. Measured value rather than an assertion: one NOT_RUN run
beside one PASS gives not_run=1, pass=1, red_ok=0, planned=2 and a pass rate of 50.0, so the NOT_RUN sits in the
denominator and never in the good count.


